Please read /papers/investigations-6/03_volterra_coefficient_dynamics/brainstorm/notes/02_VCD_OPEN_BRAINSTORM_CONTINUATION_20261115.md, I'd like to continue brainstorming about how to proceed with the induction type ideas. In addition to the ideas already described in the note, I also want to explore generally what type of inductive argument we might be looking for.&#x20;

To summarize my understanding of the program, we have a series of polynomials that are monic and orthogonal wrt the measure given by W\_\lambda, with a local recurrence defining the polynomials.  h_n defines the normalization of P_n, and adjacent ratios give coefficients \beta_n. The Volterra equations give the \lambda dependence of the \beta_n coefficients and related a_n coefficients. Also, my understanding of the J operator is that it defines multiplication by u in the orthogonal polynomial basis, and essentially decomposing the  usual exponential that appears in the definition of H\_\lambda in terms of the orthogonal polynomial basis set is what allows you to investigate the dynamics of the De Bruijn-Newman flow. Please correct any misunderstanding.

Let's now talk about the strategy for the induction. First, can you describe in more detail the idea in the brainstorm. Then, I'd like to discuss alternate general strategies. To motivate another possible line of thought, what if you truncated the series P_n series, and used an approximation for all of the terms of higher order. Perhaps this will converge for increasingly larger heights as degree increases (like in a Taylor series). If you could prove a truncated version of RH inductively, that could lead to a proof of RH. I'm not sure if that is the same as the idea you proposed.
